  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $6$ and the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}u-\\frac{1}{3}w=\\frac{1}{2} & |\\times 6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}u-\\frac{1}{2}w=\\frac{2}{5} & |\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}u-2w=3 \\\\ 2u-5w=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u-2w & =3 \\\\ u & =2w+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $u=2w+3$ into the second equation and then solve for $w:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u-5w & =4 \\\\ 2(2w+3)-5w & =4 \\\\ 4w+6-5w & =4 \\\\ -w+6 & =4 \\\\ -w & =-2 \\\\ w & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $u,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation $u=2w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2w+3 \\\\  & =2(2)+3 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $u=7$ and $w=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
